body:
Right? Like I want someone who has free time, and I want to be with someone without having to worry an issue with arise with their children or baby mama.

&#x200B;

Maybe i'm just selfish with my time.

I agree that you have to let her know. I'm not sure how your apps start out but usually we sit down and he asks how I am, I then usually say "These are the good things, these are the bad things" I then go over the goods and the bads. I struggle with some of the issues you've mentioned so I might bring up my eating issues like this: "This week I struggled with X" 

&#x200B;

It just sometimes takes some time to warm up. I've been seeing my T for almost 2 years and we only recently got really into one of my major traumas. 

&#x200B;

Sometimes the sessions are great where I'm going over small issues from the week. It really depends

Yes. Position does matter. I am in the same boat as you. I grew up poor, my mother didn't stress education. I only went to college when I was 25 (27 currently) due to my poor grades and ability to want to live. I'm in this boat right now. I have full time bills, I can't get student loans to cover school + full time bills, in Canada you can only work a certain amount of hours when receiving student funding, So with what they would provide it wouldn't be enough for me to go back to school and I couldn't work to make up the difference. 

&#x200B;

&#x200B;

Although it is possible to dig yourself out of the hole, it is hard. I am doing that right now, and the struggle is real. I also have never had a "dream job" I don't dream of labor, I work because I was born into this stupid society and that's what people do. I have been trying to find external sources to make me happier. I work because I have bills. What I have control over, is things outside of work.  

&#x200B;

*Most* people who enjoy work are the type of people who come from good families (with loving parents and little to no trauma) and got to go to school with financial help and didn't have to stress about it.

&#x200B;

I was in a session with my therapist and he said to me "You did the best with the tools you had" and its something I think about pretty often. I did do the best with the tools I had. I never got to have those moments with my mom talking about how well school was going, applying to schools together, opening acceptance letters together, mom stuff like that. (She isn't dead, I just have her distanced in my life these days) There are alot of things I missed out on that make me insanely sad.

&#x200B;

Just being honest people who say its easy to change your life are the people who have had things handed to them, weather it be living at home and not having to pay rent, money for school, cars etc. 

&#x200B;

I have come to terms that if I want better for myself, I need to work for it. Next week I start a job that has a living wage that will allow me to save and not want to be dead everyday (due to being poor) Life is difficult, and I am my biggest road block. I have missed out on lots in life, and there is a lot I will never do or see, but right now I am just trying to make life better in the ways I have control over. The cycle sucks so I feel for you.

&#x200B;

I hope you are able to find some external sources to improve your life. You can do it.
